WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.236
-We are here with
James Beard Award-winning chef

00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:05.571
Rick Martinez,
whose book -- whose cookbook,

00:00:05.571 --> 00:00:08.241
"Mi Cocina: Recipes and Rapture
From My Kitchen in Mexico,"

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:09.275
is available now.

00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:10.543
Rick, what are we
going to make today?

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:12.412
Thank you for being here.
-Thanks for having me.

00:00:12.412 --> 00:00:14.447
Well, it's the holidays.
-Yes.

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:15.548
-So we have to make tamales.

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:17.917
-Great.
-But first we have a cocktail.

00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:19.652
-This is the cocktail here.
-This is the cocktail, yes.

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:21.320
-Oh, it's very warm.
It has a warm feeling to it.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.421
-Yes, I know.

00:00:22.421 --> 00:00:24.123
So, I'm going to let you
pour the tequila.

00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:25.691
-Oh, there's --
Tequila goes in this.

00:00:25.691 --> 00:00:27.026
-Yes.
-Is this...

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:28.628
This isn't now mulled wine,
is it, right?

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:32.498
-No, this --
this is a hibiscus hot toddy.

00:00:32.498 --> 00:00:34.433
-Oh! I'm very excited.

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:37.103
-So we've got some
cinnamon, peppercorn, clove,

00:00:37.103 --> 00:00:38.270
and then, obviously tequila.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.538
-Alright. Thank you.
Here we go. Cheers.

00:00:39.538 --> 00:00:40.506
Happy holidays, everybody.

00:00:40.506 --> 00:00:43.509
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:45.144
No, this is wonderful.

00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:46.412
I've had tamales before.

00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:49.115
I've never had a brown butter
pecan tamale.

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:51.084
Is this like a dessert --
a dessert tamale?

00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:52.585
-It is a complete dessert.
I'm going to switch places.

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:53.553
-Okay. Great.
-Real quick.

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:55.255
Um, so, yeah, I love tamales.

00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:57.991
And not a lot of Americans
or Mexicans actually know

00:00:57.991 --> 00:01:00.326
that you can make sweet tamales.

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:03.429
And this is kind of inspired
by my love of

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:05.165
butter pecan ice cream.
-Okay, great.

00:01:05.165 --> 00:01:08.701
-So toasted pecans, brown
butter, and masa.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:10.937
-Alright. Great. And so how do
you go about it here?

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:13.739 line:0%
-Okay.
So I'm going to have you stir.

00:01:13.739 --> 00:01:15.141 line:0%
-Stir? Okay. Great.

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:17.543 line:0%
-This is the masa harina
and the toasted pecans.

00:01:17.543 --> 00:01:18.811 line:0%
And we have very --
-I'm doing very good so far.

00:01:18.811 --> 00:01:21.114 line:0%
-Yes, we have half and half.
-Okay.

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:23.449 line:0%
-And sweetened condensed milk.
-Great.

00:01:23.449 --> 00:01:24.950 line:0%
-Some vanilla.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.386 line:0%
We're gonna, like, play up those
kind of ice creamy flavors.

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:30.390 line:0%
And then to play up
the toasted nuts,

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:31.657 line:0%
we've got some brown butter

00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:33.059 line:0%
because also I
love brown butter.

00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:35.127 line:0%
-Alright.
This is very good, very light.

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:37.329 line:0%
-Yeah, doesn't it seem light?
-It does seem light.

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:38.598 line:0%
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-It does. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.266 line:0%
-Okay. So, yeah, really, really,
really get in there.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:41.334 line:0%
-Gotcha.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:42.435 line:0%
-But I could really,
really do this,

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:43.736 line:0%
but I can also
tell someone already has,

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:44.670 line:0%
so why don't we skip
to that part?

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.272 line:0%
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:47.506 line:0%
-You know,
who are we doing here, right?

00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:50.343 line:0%
It's not CGI.
They all can see it.

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:52.211 line:0%
-Okay. So, now
we're going to roll them up.

00:01:52.211 --> 00:01:53.646 line:0%
So I'll do one for you.

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:54.980 line:0%
Grab grab a...
-Alright. Great.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:58.417 line:0%
And while we roll,
I want to ask about the book

00:01:58.417 --> 00:01:59.853 line:0%
because one of
the things you did,

00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:01.854 line:0%
I think the biggest mistake
anybody could make is saying,

00:02:01.854 --> 00:02:03.289 line:0%
"Oh, Mexican food
is one thing."

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:06.425 line:0%
You went to all
the different regions and --

00:02:06.425 --> 00:02:07.793 line:0%
and was that
a fun journey for you?

00:02:07.793 --> 00:02:09.428 line:0%
Did you know it all,
or did you learn stuff?

00:02:09.428 --> 00:02:11.664 line:0%
-No. I grew up in Texas,
and I grew up

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:12.665 line:0%
thinking that --
-Whoo!

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:13.599 line:0%
-Whoo-hoo!

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:15.301 line:0%
[ Chuckles ] Hook 'em.
-Yeah.

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:18.003 line:0%
-So I grew up thinking
that we ate Mexican food,

00:02:18.003 --> 00:02:21.174 line:0%
you know, like, and really,
it was Tex-Mex and, um...

00:02:21.174 --> 00:02:22.742 line:0%
Sorry. Okay.
-No, no. This is --

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.443 line:0%
What I'm doing is good.
-Yeah, yeah. And then just --

00:02:24.443 --> 00:02:26.311 line:0%
[ Laughs ]
It's perfect. It's beautiful.

00:02:26.311 --> 00:02:27.413 line:0%
Exactly.
-Oh, my God. Look at that.

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:29.782 line:0%
[ Stammering ]
-There you go.

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:33.419 line:0%
Um, I grew up thinking that
we ate Mexican food, and so I...

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:35.354 line:0%
When I started
traveling in Mexico,

00:02:35.354 --> 00:02:37.990 line:0%
I was like, "This is nothing
like what we ate."

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.325 line:0%
-Right.
-And so I just wanted

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:42.228 line:0%
to explore, and so I bought
a car in Mexico City

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:44.831 line:0%
and literally drove
about 30,000 miles

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:47.499 line:0%
exploring the entire country,
every state.

00:02:47.499 --> 00:02:50.068 line:0%
-That's amazing.
-Yeah. Eating my way around.

00:02:50.068 --> 00:02:52.938 line:0%
-And now tell me, there's a --
I think a lot of times

00:02:52.938 --> 00:02:55.074 line:0%
people use
the word authentic, uh,

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:57.142 line:0%
to refer to foods
from different countries.

00:02:57.142 --> 00:02:58.578 line:0%
You do not care
for the word authentic.

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:02.614 line:0%
-I actually hate that word,
because I feel like,

00:03:02.614 --> 00:03:04.017 line:0%
like in this country,

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:07.287 line:0%
Mexican cooks
cook with what they love,

00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:09.722 line:0%
with what they have,
with what's in season.

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:13.226 line:0%
And so here we talk about
authentic as being like,

00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:15.595 line:0%
this is the one way
that you make tamales.

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:18.598 line:0%
And so I started exploring
the country of Mexico

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:21.200 line:0%
and thinking I'm going to find
that one version of pozole,

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.302 line:0%
that one version
of the tamale,

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:27.273 line:0%
and actually, no,
everybody just cooks for fun.

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:29.442 line:0%
It's their creative outlet.

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:31.010 line:0%
If you invite 10 people
over to your house

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.345 line:0%
and you ask them
to make the same dish,

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:33.613 line:0%
you're going to have 10

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:36.048 line:0%
amazingly different
versions of it.

00:03:36.048 --> 00:03:38.585 line:0%
And so I feel like, you know,

00:03:38.585 --> 00:03:40.286 line:0%
we shouldn't -- we don't
need that word.

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:42.321 line:0%
-Yeah. Give yourself
the freedom to explore outside

00:03:42.321 --> 00:03:43.422 line:0%
of whatever authentic is.

00:03:43.422 --> 00:03:44.791 line:0%
Alright.
Now show me what we do here.

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:46.992 line:0%
-Okay.
-Very excited.

00:03:46.992 --> 00:03:49.429 line:0%
-So this is actually
my favorite moment.

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:51.798 line:0%
This is the moment when you've
been waiting for 45 minutes.

00:03:51.798 --> 00:03:53.066 line:0%
We've steamed these tamales

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:56.535 line:0%
and now we have the reveal.
-Oh, my goodness.

00:03:56.535 --> 00:04:00.072 line:0%
-Yes.
-Oh. Yes.

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:01.407 line:0%
-Oh, they really smell good.
Alright.

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:04.877 line:0%
-Yeah, yeah. So -- But...
-They're so hot.

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:06.045 line:0%
-I actually have...

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:07.646 line:0%
-Why did I stick
my hand in there?

00:04:07.646 --> 00:04:09.381 line:0%
-Because it's fun.
-Okay, great. Oh, see?

00:04:09.381 --> 00:04:10.483 line:0%
-For you.
-That's when you got to go,

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:12.385 line:0%
"Stop, stop,
we made it already."

00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:14.020 line:0%
-Exactly.
-Okay, great.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:15.888 line:0%
-And to gild the lily
a little bit,

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:19.158 line:0%
I added some dulce de leche
and some extra toasted pecans.

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.761 line:0%
-This looks fantastic.
I'm very excited, Rick.

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:23.262 line:0%
-Alright. Cheers.

00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:24.897 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:26.132 line:0%
Oh, well. Yeah, yeah.

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:28.433 line:0%
[ Laughs ]
-Mmm!

00:04:28.433 --> 00:04:30.736 line:0%
This is amazing, everybody!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:30.736 --> 00:04:33.806 line:0%
Give it up
for Chef Rick Martinez!

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:37.276 line:0%
"Mi Cocina: Recipes and Rapture
From My Kitchen in Mexico"

00:04:37.276 --> 00:04:38.477 line:0%
is available now.

00:04:38.477 --> 00:04:39.579 line:0%
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

